description:
Gives one book's full ReadingRate analysis and shows its difficulty profile as a radar chart: nine difficulty dimensions and four literary-regard dimensions, each scored 0 to 100 with a written reason, plus the book's description, genres, themes, word count, reading time, authors, awards and editions. awards holds prizes and canonical lists; taught_in holds the school curricula and exam boards that set the book, which are not awards.

Use when the user asks how hard a specific book is, what makes it difficult, how long it takes to read, whether it has won prizes, or for details about it. To find books that match a description, use search_books. To set two or three books side by side, use compare_books.

Identify the book with exactly one of book_id, slug or title_author.

Difficulty dimensions: vocabulary_complexity, syntactic_complexity, abstraction_level and information_density (how hard the prose is); narrative_indirection, symbolic_density, ironic_distance, resolution_withholding and structural_complexity (how hard the meaning is). Regard dimensions: canonical_status, critical_acclaim, literary_influence, author_standing.

Examples:
- {"slug": "crime-and-punishment"}
- {"title_author": {"title": "The Great Gatsby", "author": "Fitzgerald"}}
- {"slug": "beloved", "include_similar": true}
- {"slug": "war-and-peace", "include_time_estimate": true}
